- Could compiler intrinsics offer portable fallbacks here?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design branchless bit tricks for palindrome detection on 64-bit values, a common bitboard problem.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR-based bit reversal
- Branchless boolean results
- Fixed-width masking patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
function reverse64(n) {
  n = ((n >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  n = ((n >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  n = ((n >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n);
  n = ((n >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n);
  n = ((n >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) | ((n & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n);
  n = (n >> 32n) | (n << 32n);
  return n;
}
function isPalindrome64(n) {
  return reverse64(n) === n;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless 128-bit rotation using only shifts.

## Key Concepts
- View 128-bit value via 128-bit type
- Branchless rotation with shifts and modulo
- Correct unpacking into hi/lo

## Code Example
```c
void rotateRight128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  unsigned int kk = k & 127;
  __uint128_t v = (((__uint128_t)hi) << 64) | lo;
  v = (v >> kk) | (v << ((128 - kk) % 128));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(v >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)v;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement without 128-bit type support?
- What are performance trade-offs for different compilers?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to implement conditional data movement without branching. It demonstrates how to construct a full-word mask from a single flag and apply it consistently across 128-bit halves.

## Key Concepts
- **Branchless conditioning via masks:** Using arithmetic to convert a boolean into an all-0s or all-1s mask
- **128-bit representation:** A 128-bit value split into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- **Portable unsigned arithmetic:** Safe, well-defined behavior across platforms

## Code Example
```c
uint128 swapHalvesIf128(uint128 x, unsigned c) {
    unsigned m = -(unsigned)(c != 0);
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi & ~m) | (x.lo & m);
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo & ~m) | (x.hi & m);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests non-branching bit-twiddling and packing/unpacking across a mask, a common performance pattern in high-throughput code.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masks and order-preserving packing
- Non-branching compression via cascade masks
- Fixed-sequence bit-manipulation tricks

## Code Example
```javascript
function bitpack64(v, mask){
  let x = v & mask;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 1n)) & 0x5555555555555555n;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 2n)) & 0x3333333333333333n;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 8n)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 16n)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 32n)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFn;
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support dynamic masks without recompilation?
- Compare performance with a loop-based approach on large data sets.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level bitboard thinking, edge masking, and 128-bit manipulation without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 8x16 bitboard mapping in 128 bits (bit i = row*16 + col).
- Horizontal moves use 1-bit shifts with edge masks (COL0/COL15).
- Vertical moves use 16-bit shifts with TOP/BOTTOM masks.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode close to C for clarity
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 expandMask128(uint128 m){
  uint128 left  = (m & ~COL0) >> 1;
  uint128 right = (m & ~COL15) << 1;
  uint128 up    = (m & ~TOP) << 16;
  uint128 down  = (m & ~BOTTOM) >> 16;
  return or128(or128(left, right), or128(up, down));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for an irregular 3x5 tile grid embedded in 128 bits?
- What are the performance implications of branching-free bitboard ops on modern CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
Branchless 128-bit comparison is common in crypto/hash pipelines and requires careful borrow logic across halves. It tests low-level reasoning without relying on 128-bit native ops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit numbers split into hi/lo.
- Borrow propagation across 64-bit halves.
- Branchless evaluation using comparisons on unsigned integers.

## Code Example
```c
uint8_t lessThan128(uint128 a, uint128 b){
  uint64_t loDiff = a.lo - b.lo;
  uint64_t borrowLo = (a.lo < b.lo);
  uint64_t hiDiff = a.hi - b.hi - borrowLo;
  uint64_t borrowHi = (a.hi < b.hi + borrowLo);
  return (uint8_t)borrowHi;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to extend to 128-bit equality without extra branches?
- How would you adapt for signed 128-bit comparison?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply SWAR bit counting to 128-bit data without branches. Also checks splitting 128-bit into halves and combining results efficiently.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR bitcount technique for 64-bit words
- Handling 128-bit data via hi/lo halves
- Branchless arithmetic for performance

## Code Example
```javascript
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t v) {
  v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333);
  v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0f;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101) >> 56;
}
```
Then: hiCount = popcount64(x.hi); loCount = popcount64(x.lo); return hiCount + loCount;

